Title:
How Do You Investigate Windows Event Logs? (Windows Event Log Analysis 101)

Type:
Blog Post

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
This post is a DFIR/incident-response oriented primer on investigating Windows Event Logs, emphasizing hypothesis-driven analysis rather than “find all suspicious events.” It explains key limitations (incomplete coverage, audit policy dependencies, log size/overwrite, attacker log clearing/tampering) and stresses rapid preservation of EVTX before retention wipes evidence. The author outlines how to define investigation purpose and scope (hosts, time window, channels, users, behaviors) to reduce noise in multi‑GB log sets. It provides a practical starting set of high-value Event IDs across authentication, Kerberos/NTLM, RDP, process creation, PowerShell, WMI, SMB lateral movement, services, scheduled tasks, account/group changes, firewall/Defender, and log tampering/rotation. It also covers tooling workflows (Event Viewer vs wevtutil export, EVTX parsing to CSV/JSON with EvtxECmd/evtx2es, analysis with Timeline Explorer/Elasticsearch/DuckDB, Sigma scanners like Chainsaw/Hayabusa/Zircolite) and cautions on using generative AI safely and skeptically. Useful primarily for IR analysts/blue teams, but also valuable to red teams for understanding what artifacts common tradecraft leaves behind.

Technical Focus:
- Windows auditing/audit policy and EVTX retention/rotation behavior
- High-signal Windows Event IDs for intrusion timelines (logon, process, PowerShell, SMB, tasks, services)
- Kerberos/NTLM and domain controller authentication telemetry
- RDP/TerminalServices operational logs for session tracing
- Log preservation/export and parsing pipelines (wevtutil, EvtxECmd, JSONL, Elasticsearch/DuckDB)
- Sigma-based hunting over EVTX (Chainsaw/Hayabusa/Zircolite) and validation practices

Use Cases:
- Rapid triage of suspected Windows intrusion using a minimal set of Event IDs
- Building an authentication/lateral-movement timeline (4624/4625/4648, 4768/4769/4776, RDP logs)
- Detecting persistence via services (4697/7045), scheduled tasks (4698+), and WMI subscriptions (5859/5861)
- Identifying log clearing/tampering and retention gaps (1102/104, 1104/1105)
- Converting EVTX to CSV/JSONL and querying at scale with Timeline Explorer, Elasticsearch, or DuckDB
- Running Sigma rule scans over collected EVTX to prioritize deeper manual analysis

Keywords:
Windows Event Log, EVTX, DFIR, incident response, audit policy, log retention, log rotation, wevtutil, Event Viewer, Security.evtx, Sysmon, Event ID 4624, 4625, 4688, 4768, 4769, 4776, 1102, RDP, TerminalServices, Kerberos, NTLM, SMB, scheduled tasks, WMI persistence, Microsoft Defender Operational, Windows Firewall, EvtxECmd, Chainsaw, Hayabusa, Zircolite, Sigma, Elasticsearch, DuckDB, JSONL